course: everyday
lesson: 84
title: Bruises, head bumps, nosebleeds and burns
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A bruise is bleeding under the skin from tiny broken blood vessels; cold, wrapped in a cloth, makes the vessels narrower, so there is less swelling and pain.
explain: The brain is soft and sits inside the hard skull; after a hard knock it can be hurt even if you feel fine at first, so an adult needs to know and watch you.
explain: For a nosebleed you lean forward, not back, and pinch the soft part of your nose; for a burn you cool it under cool running water for 20 minutes.
explain: A girl in Perth touches a hot oven tray; she runs her hand under the cool tap for 20 minutes, tells her mum, and does not pop the blister that forms later.
fact: Put a cold pack wrapped in a cloth on a bruise for a few minutes, because cold reduces swelling and pain.
fact: Never put ice straight on bare skin, because it can damage the skin.
fact: After a hard bump on the head, tell an adult even if you feel fine, because a head injury needs watching.
fact: Feeling sleepy, sick, dizzy or confused after a head bump can be a sign of concussion (a brain injury from a knock), so you need a doctor straight away.
fact: For a nosebleed, sit down, lean forward and pinch the soft part of your nose for about 10 minutes, breathing through your mouth.
fact: Do not tilt your head back in a nosebleed, because blood runs down your throat and can make you sick.
fact: A nosebleed that will not stop after 20 minutes, or that came after a big knock, needs medical help.
fact: After a nosebleed, do not blow or pick your nose for a while, so the clot can heal.
fact: Cool a burn under cool running water for 20 minutes and tell an adult, because cooling stops the burn going deeper.
fact: Never put butter, toothpaste or ice on a burn, because they can make it worse or cause infection.
fact: Do not pop a blister from a burn, because it protects the skin underneath from infection.
fact: A big burn, or a burn on the face or hands, needs an adult to call for medical help.
exercise: You bump your leg on a table and it starts to bruise. What helps? | A cold pack wrapped in a cloth for a few minutes | A hot water bottle pressed on it for an hour or more | Rubbing it hard to spread the bruise | Pressing it to see if it hurts
exercise: Why should ice be wrapped in a cloth before it touches skin? | Ice on bare skin can damage it | The cloth makes the ice colder | Ice melts too slowly without a cloth | Bare ice makes the bruise spread
exercise: You hit your head hard on the climbing frame but feel fine. What should you do? | Tell an adult anyway | Say nothing because you feel fine | Go straight back up the frame | Have a sleep until home time
exercise: After a head bump you feel dizzy and sick. What does this mean? | You need a doctor; it may be concussion | You are just hungry and need a sugary snack to feel better | It is normal and will pass in a minute | You should run to clear your head
exercise: Your nose starts to bleed. What should you do? | Sit, lean forward and pinch the soft part | Lie down flat on the floor | Tip your head right back and look up at the ceiling | Blow your nose hard to clear it
exercise: Why should you not tip your head back in a nosebleed? | Blood runs down the throat and can make you sick | It makes the nose bleed from both sides at the same time | It stops you breathing through your mouth | It makes the blood flow faster
exercise: How long should you pinch your nose for a nosebleed? | About 10 minutes | About 10 seconds | About one hour | Until you sneeze
exercise: A nosebleed has not stopped after 20 minutes. What now? | Get medical help | Keep pinching for the rest of the day | Stuff it with toilet paper and play | Lie down and wait for it to stop
exercise: Your nose has just stopped bleeding. What should you avoid for a while? | Blowing or picking your nose | Drinking cold water | Talking and laughing with your friends | Breathing through your nose
exercise: You touch a hot iron. What is the right first aid? | Cool it under cool running water for 20 minutes | Put a thick layer of butter on it straight away | Hold an ice cube on it | Wrap it tightly and leave it
exercise: Someone says to put toothpaste on your burn. What do you do? | Use cool running water instead | Use mint toothpaste only, as it cools | Put on a thick layer of it | Mix it with butter first
exercise: A blister forms on your burn. What should you do? | Leave it and do not pop it | Pop it with a pin to drain it | Peel off the top skin | Squeeze it until it is flat
exercise: Your little sister spills hot tea on her face. What is right? | Cool it and get an adult to call for medical help | Put a plaster on it and wait | Rub some cream on it quickly and keep playing as before | Put ice right on her skin
test: A boy in Glasgow falls and bangs his head; an hour later he is confused. What should happen? | An adult takes him to a doctor straight away | He should go to bed and sleep it off until the morning | He should do some maths to test himself | Nothing, because it was an hour ago
test: Which nosebleed advice is right? | Lean forward and pinch the soft part of the nose | Lean back and pinch the hard, bony top part of the nose | Lie down and hold your breath | Put an ice cube up your nose
test: Why does cooling a burn for a long time help? | It stops the heat going deeper into the skin | It washes all the germs off the burn | It slowly makes the burn turn into a small bruise | It numbs the skin so you forget it
test: A friend has a frozen bag of peas for her bruised arm. How should she use it? | Wrap it in a tea towel and hold it on | Hold the bare bag on for an hour | Put it on, then on a hot pack straight after | Open it and rub the peas on the arm
test: Grandpa says to put butter on a burn, as people did long ago. What is true? | Butter can make burns worse; use cool water | Butter is still the best first aid for any burn | Butter works if you add salt | Butter is fine for small burns only
test: Why is a head bump watched even if the child seems fine? | Signs of brain injury can come later | Bumps always make children lie about how they feel | Adults need to measure the bump | Head bumps turn into colds
test: A girl's burn has a big blister. Why should it be left alone? | It protects the skin underneath from infection | Popping it makes the burn hotter | Blisters always go away by themselves in an hour | It will become a scab if left long
test: Which burn needs a doctor? | A big burn or a burn on the face or hands | A tiny burn on the elbow that was cooled for twenty minutes | A warm feeling after a hot bath | Warm hands after holding cocoa
test: A boy's nose bled after a football hit him hard in the face. What should he do? | Get medical help, because it came after a big knock | Keep playing and pinch it at half time | Blow his nose hard to stop it | Tip his head back until it stops
test: nosebleed!! tip my head back? | No, sit, lean forward and pinch the soft part | Yes, tip your head right back and look up | Yes, and lie down flat | Yes, and blow your nose hard
test: burnt my hand on the oven, get the butter | No, cool it under running water for 20 minutes | Yes, butter cools the burn | Put some toothpaste on it instead, it's minty | Put ice right on the skin
test: banged my head hard at the park but im fine | Tell an adult anyway, a head injury needs watching | If you feel fine there's no need to tell anyone | Go to sleep straight away | Only tell if it bleeds
test: blister came up on the burn, pop it? | No, it protects the skin from infection | Yes, pop it so it heals faster | Yes, with a clean needle from the sewing box | Yes, and peel the skin off
explain: A boy in Manchester put butter on a burn from the kettle; it kept the heat in and the burn got worse, and at the hospital the nurse said cool running water would have helped.
explain: A girl in Texas hit her head hard falling off a trampoline and said she was fine; later she was sleepy and sick, and doctors found a concussion.
fact: A burn should be cooled under cool running water for 20 minutes; butter, ice and creams make it worse.
fact: After a hard knock to the head, being very sleepy, being sick or having a bad headache are signs to see a doctor at once.
test: burnt my hand on the pan, nan says butter it | No, cool it under cool running water for 20 minutes | Yes, butter takes the heat out | Put ice straight on for an hour | Pop the blister so it heals fast
test: bonked my head on the trampoline, now feel sick n sleepy | Tell an adult now, you may need a doctor | Go to sleep and it'll pass | Keep jumping to shake it off | Eat something sweet and forget it
test: nosebleed!! tip my head back? | No, lean forward and pinch the soft part of the nose | Yes, tip it right back | Lie flat on your back | Blow your nose really hard
test: omg Jake's nose bled at lunch for like an HOUR, there was a puddle on the floor, he tipped his head back n still nothing | Lean forward and pinch the soft part; the puddle is likely exaggerated, and one over 20 minutes needs help | Tipping the head back is right, he just did not do it long enough | All nosebleeds last an hour, so nothing needs doing | He should blow his nose hard to clear it out
test: my nan says put butter on a burn, her mum always did, it's like a secret trick | No, cool a burn under cool running water for 20 minutes; butter makes it worse | Yes, old tricks from grandparents are always the safest | Yes, but only salted butter works on burns | Toothpaste is the real trick, not butter
test: heard Mia hit her head on the climbing frame n now she's "brain damaged" lol, pass it on | Do not pass it on; it is cruel rumour, and head bumps need an adult to watch | Pass it on, everyone should know who is hurt | Pass it on, but only to her best friends | Pass it on, because it is funny if she is fine
exam: Which of the following procedures is recommended for the management of an epistaxis, hereinafter referred to as a nosebleed? | The person sits, leans forward and pinches the soft part of the nose for about ten minutes | The person lies flat and tilts the head backwards until the bleeding has ceased | The person blows the nose forcefully at intervals so as to expel all clotted blood | The person places a quantity of ice directly upon the bare skin of the nose
exam: Determine the correct treatment of a burn sustained to the skin in a domestic kitchen. | Cooling under cool running water for twenty minutes, an adult having been informed | The application of butter or toothpaste immediately after the injury has occurred | The deliberate rupture of any blister in order that the skin may breathe freely | The placement of ice directly upon the burn for as long as it can be tolerated
exam: Subsequent to a hard blow to the head, which of the following signs necessitates the attendance of a physician at once? | Marked sleepiness, vomiting, dizziness, confusion or a severe headache | A small and painless swelling which has already reduced after a cold pack | Mild embarrassment at having struck one's head in the presence of others | A desire to resume play which persists after the incident has concluded
exam: State the reason for which ice ought not to be applied directly to the bare skin in the treatment of a bruise. | Ice in direct contact may damage the skin, so a cold pack is wrapped in a cloth | Ice applied directly causes the bruise to spread to adjoining parts of the body | Ice in direct contact makes the bruise permanent and prevents it from fading | Ice applied directly is ineffective, as cold has no effect upon any swelling
